    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Natural gas and crude oil reserves appear to be extensive in the Arctic. The complexity of the transportation of both oil and gas from these areas to markets in temperate climates has been a major obstacle to the exploitation of this energy resource. A new method of transporting both crude oil and natural gas in a single carrier was investigated. An all-liquid system v/as proposed that consists of crude oil dispersed in methanol which can be readily synthesized from natural gas. This crude oil-in-methanol dispersion was developed with the aid of commercial stabilizing agents. Stable oil-in-methanol dispersions likely suitable for pipeline transportation were produced in the laboratory. Rheological measurements were then taken in order to predict the flow properties of such dispersion in pipelines.The feasibility of transporting the oil-in-methanol dispersion in an adaptation of the Trans-Alaska pipeline was evaluated in an engineering study. The analysis indicated that the transportation of oil-in methanol-dispersions in the Trans-Alaska pipeline was technically feasible at design flow rates. The efficiencies and the economics of transporting oil-in-methanol dispersions in the modified pipeline were compared with those for the various proposed pipelines for transporting Prudhoe Bay crude oil and natural gas separately to the lower 49 states. The energy efficiency of the dispersion system was appreciably less than the combined efficiencies of the three proposed oil and gas transportation systems. The capital investment of $3 billion required to modify the Trans-Alaska pipeline was significantly less than the proposed construction cost, $6 and $10 billion, for the natural gas systems. The incremental operating expense for the dispersion system was slightly more than the operating expenses of the natural gas systems.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
